Fulgurite Skank
Ingredients: NU JAZZ / ELECTRO JAZZ + (ROOTS) REGGAE + HYPNOTIC, TRANCE-LIKE & MINIMAL + Brass
Creative Catalyst: A phantom offshore pirate radio station that only transmitted when lightning strikes grounded directly into its copper sea-tower.

Fulgurite Skank sounds like a dub temple built inside an offshore squall: massive, glacial roots-reggae sub-basslines moving at half-speed, decoupled from sunny tropes and dragged into hypnotic, trance-like minimalism. Instead of bright offbeat skanks, brass instruments—stifled by custom leather and salt-crusted metal mutes—breathe long, circular-breathing drones and clipped, microtonal jazz stabs that mimic thunder rolling across open water. It is cool, oceanic, crackling with electrostatic air, and relentlessly cyclical.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 64–72 BPM. The traditional reggae "one-drop" is hollowed out into a hypnotic, unyielding motorik half-time crawl, anchored by deep, sub-aquatic kick thuds and an absent snare that lets the dead space linger.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Phrygian modal jazz clusters; low-register open fifths; dissonance tuned to barometric resonance rather than Western twelve-tone temperaments; eerie, sustained natural-minor brass chord sheets.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Subverted Horn Channelling: Brass is never played as brassy fanfares or virtuoso solos; trumpets and baritone horns are played via circular breathing through cup mutes stuffed with wet sea-sponge, functioning as oscillating sub-rhythm generators.
  2. Cold-War Tape Saturation: Dub delays are calibrated to long, disintegrating tape loops that decay into low-frequency rumbles rather than bright slapbacks.
  3. Galvanic Arc Saturation (Impossible Technique): The master audio signal is routed across a spark-gap between twin lightning rods mounted over salt-water vats, allowing atmospheric ionization during active thunderstorms to physically re-magnetize the master tape heads with unpredictable surges of raw electrical crunch.
- Dynamics: Glacial, cyclical architecture. Tracks do not rely on standard drops or pop structures; they accumulate atmospheric weight through slowly cycling 16-bar brass loops and deep bass undulations that swell, crack open in a flash of filtered white noise, and recede like retreating storm surges.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement coalesced on the squall-battered cliffs of Morant Bay, Jamaica, where a clandestine broadcast collective called Station 108.4 Ceraunia operated from an abandoned limestone lighthouse. The station possessed no generators; its rudimentary vacuum-tube transmitter was wired to a massive copper rod that drew juice solely from direct lightning strikes, broadcasting ten-to-fifteen-minute transmissions of hypnotic, horn-heavy dub-jazz before plunging into silence. A subculture of "Storm Steppers" emerged in coastal rum shacks, tuning shortwave radios with feverish devotion whenever barometer readings crashed. Fashion within the scene rejected tropical leisure: dancers wear oversized, rubberized black fisherman slickers, amber-tinted industrial glassmaker goggles, and heavy rubber boots, gathering in darkened open-air dancehalls to move in slow, trance-locked circles while rain lashes the corrugated zinc roofs.

Central Instruments: Baritone saxophone, muted bass trombone, flugelhorn, sub-harmonic electric bass, Fender Rhodes running through ring modulators, vintage Roland RE-201 Space Echo, and lightning-triggered tape loops.

For Fans Of: Moritz von Oswald Trio, The Skatalites (slowed down to 33 RPM), Sons of Kemet, Rhythm & Sound

