Track 2: Sister Orla’s Cracked Marver
- Story & Context: Technician Kael spent three weeks isolated on Lime Cay patching damaged radio valves with crushed green cullet melted over a kerosene burner, nursing third-degree palm burns that smelled of bitter flux and iodine. When patrol cutters combed the dark reef looking for the bootleg frequency, he refused to cut the transmitter switch, instead muttering confessions into the open bell of his bass trombone while rain flooded his battery bank. His lone horn note looped for twenty minutes straight, a slow, microtonal prayer cutting through the hypnotic static of the squall. He watched his reflection shatter in the oil-streaked rainwater pooling across his worktable and whispered to the console: "Every fracture in the lime holds a pulse we couldn't hide."
