Track 3: Cathode Vitrification at Noon
- Story & Context: Decades after the sea-tower antenna took its final fatal strike, beachcombers still scour the cliffs of Morant Point for strange, hollow fulgurite tubes formed where lightning fused coastal sand directly through the copper grounding cables. Local sound-system crews drag analog delays out to the rocky ledge every hurricane season, aiming directional microphones at the whistling green silica formations to harvest phantom sub-bass frequencies born from the rocks themselves. Old men in the rum shops swear that on humid afternoons, the stones still hum the phantom radio station's opening horn signature as the tide rolls back over the reef. A fading chalk inscription scrawled upon the surviving concrete footing still reads: "What the lightning turned to glass, no cold tide can wash clean."
